WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.584
Rust security improves when teams build
feedback into the development and release

2
00:00:04.664 --> 00:00:05.131
process.

3
00:00:05.211 --> 00:00:08.537
Unit tests, integration tests, negative
tests, regression tests,

4
00:00:08.617 --> 00:00:12.643
and property-style tests help verify that
expected behavior remains true as code

5
00:00:12.723 --> 00:00:13.190
changes.

6
00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:16.654
Tests should cover success paths,
rejection paths, boundary cases,

7
00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:20.702
authorization decisions, configuration
validation, and known failure modes.

8
00:00:20.782 --> 00:00:24.283
Fuzzing concepts are useful defensively,
especially around parsers,

9
00:00:24.363 --> 00:00:28.272
deserializers, file formats, protocol
handling, and other input-heavy code.

10
00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:31.736
The goal is to discover unexpected
behavior before production does.

11
00:00:31.816 --> 00:00:33.800
Fuzzing does not replace design review,

12
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.623
but it can reveal assumptions that
ordinary tests miss.

13
00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:40.962
Logging and observability should help
teams understand what the application is doing

14
00:00:41.042 --> 00:00:43.784
without exposing secrets or excessive
sensitive data.

15
00:00:43.864 --> 00:00:46.490
Structured logs can support authentication
review,

16
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:50.013
authorization decisions, validation
failures, dependency issues,

17
00:00:50.093 --> 00:00:52.310
performance limits, and unexpected errors.

18
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:56.533
Redaction and event design matter because
logs often travel farther and live longer

19
00:00:56.613 --> 00:00:57.838
than developers expect.

20
00:00:57.918 --> 00:01:01.477
Release hardening includes reviewing build
profiles, debug settings,

21
00:01:01.557 --> 00:01:04.650
feature flags, dependency records,
SBOM-style inventories,

22
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.639
configuration defaults, deployment
documentation, and incident readiness.

23
00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:12.512
Security is easier to sustain when the
application is testable, observable,

24
00:01:12.592 --> 00:01:13.409
and reviewable.

25
00:01:13.489 --> 00:01:17.806
A strong release process turns Rust's
language-level strengths into an operationally

26
00:01:17.886 --> 00:01:18.820
reliable product.
